//EVMC has no inline assembly due to the difficulties it could potentially
//pose for the optimizer and Core IR.
//Structured control flow with locals on the stack is well-suited for most EVM
//use cases, but not all of them; in particular, automata which push or pop
//a variable number of words cannot be implemented directly in EVMC.
//Transparent closures are a particularly compelling use case for such
//automata, so I'll do my best to get something like assembly using EVMC's
//existing features.

//Design:
//Assembly sections can be placed in code using code globals; as long as
//they're all well-formed bytecode (i.e. none contain a PUSH* that consumes
//bytes off the end), they should be executable.
//Structs can be used to tag basic blocks with labels.
//For that I need to serialize &(codeG.field), requiring static eval of
//&_ and *_.
//Oh no: consider a single-BB while loop.
//code codeG = Struct(Jumpdest,...,PushPtr (&codeG), Jumpi).
//That forms an SCC; can HM handle that? Worse, datatypes can't hide the type
//params of their arguments because I don't have existentials.
//Solution: support serialization of coerce e.
//Need to be careful to ensure supported compile-time eval remains consistent
//with runtime behavior, modulo Code allocValue.
//Instructions translate to mnemonic datatypes which have the opcode as their
//tag.

//A datatype of EVM opcodes, not including immediate arguments.
//Algo: intercalate ";\n " uppercase mnemonics in ascending order of opcode.
//GHCI in compiler repo:
//:l OpcodeInfo
//import Data.Char
//import Data.List (sort,intercalate)
//op_mnems = sort $ map (\(mnem,op)->(op, map toUpper mnem)) $
//           M.toList $ M.map oiOpcode opcodes
//writeFile "toCopy.txt" $ intercalate ";\n" [" "++mnem | (_,mnem) <- op_mnems]
data EVM = {
 STOP;
 ADD;
 MUL;
 SUB;
 DIV;
 SDIV;
 MOD;
 SMOD;
 ADDMOD;
 MULMOD;
 EXP;
 SIGNEXTEND;
 LT;
